<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <section xml:id="sec-section-1-3">
      <title>Section 03 - Unit Vectors</title>

<introduction>
<objectives>
  <p><em>Learning Objectives</em></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Find a unit vector.</li>
    <li>Express a vector in terms of standard unit vectors.</li>
  </ul>
</objectives>
</introduction>

      <subsection xml:id="sec-03-01-unit-vector">
        <title>Find a Unit Vector</title>
        <p>
  We now introduce a normalized version of a vector that captures direction without magnitude.
</p>

<definition>
  <title>Unit Vector</title>

  <p>
    A <em>unit vector</em> is a vector with magnitude <m>1</m>.
    For any nonzero vector <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, the unit vector
    <m>\mathbf{u}</m> in the direction of <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is defined by
  </p>

  <me>
    \mathbf{u} = \frac{\mathbf{v}}{\lVert \mathbf{v} \rVert}
  </me>
</definition>
<worksheet xml:id="unit-vector-concept-check">
  <title>Concept Check</title>

  <exercise xml:id="unit-vector-concept-check-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Answer using words or a sketch, not calculations.
      </p>

      <ol marker="A.">
        <li>
          <p>
            What information about a vector is preserved when we convert it to a unit vector?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            What information is lost when a vector is converted to a unit vector?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Can two different vectors produce the same unit vector?
            Explain your reasoning.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>
<worksheet xml:id="unit-vector-examples">
  <title>In Class Example</title>

  <exercise xml:id="unit-vector-examples-ex-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        For the vector <m>\langle 3,-4 \rangle</m>, find a unit vector.
        Before computing, predict whether the unit vector will point into
        Quadrant II, III, or IV.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="unit-vector-examples-ex-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\mathbf{v}=\langle -4,1 \rangle</m>.
        Find the unit vector in the opposite direction of <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Before computing, describe in words how the direction of this unit vector
        compares to the direction of <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
      </subsection>
      <subsection xml:id="sec-03-04-standard-unit-vectors">
        <title>Express a Vector in Terms of Standard Unit Vectors</title>
<p>
  It is often useful to express vectors using fixed reference directions along the coordinate axes.
</p>

<definition>
  <title>Standard Unit Vectors</title>

  <p>
    If <m>\mathbf{v}=\langle x,y\rangle</m>, then <m>\mathbf{v}</m> can be written in
    standard unit vector form as
  </p>

  <me>
    \mathbf{v}=x\mathbf{i}+y\mathbf{j}
  </me>

  <p>
    where the <em>standard unit vectors</em> are
    <m>\mathbf{i}=\langle 1,0\rangle</m> and
    <m>\mathbf{j}=\langle 0,1\rangle</m>.
  </p>
</definition>
<worksheet xml:id="standard-unit-vectors-examples">
  <title>In Class Example</title>

  <exercise xml:id="standard-unit-vectors-examples-ex-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Express <m>\mathbf{v}=\langle 4,-8\rangle</m> in terms of standard unit vectors.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="standard-unit-vectors-examples-ex-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\mathbf{v}</m> be a unit vector that forms an angle of
        <m>330^\circ</m> with the positive <m>x</m>-axis.
        Express <m>\mathbf{v}</m> in terms of standard unit vectors.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="standard-unit-vectors-examples-ex-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\mathbf{v}=-\mathbf{i}+2\mathbf{j}</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{w}=-4\mathbf{i}-3\mathbf{j}</m>.
        Express <m>\mathbf{v}-\mathbf{w}</m> in terms of standard unit vectors.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
      </subsection>

      <subsection xml:id="sec-03-06-groupwork">
        <title>Groupwork Activity</title>
<worksheet xml:id="unit-vectors-groupwork">
  <title>Exploring Unit Vectors Geometrically</title>
  <exercise xml:id="unit-vectors-groupwork-ex-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Work with a partner to answer the following questions.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Draw two different vectors that have the same unit vector.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Draw a vector and its corresponding unit vector on the same set of axes.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Can the zero vector have a unit vector?
            Explain why or why not.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        Be prepared to justify your answers without using formulas.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>
      </subsection>
<subsection>
<title>Exercises on Unit Vectors</title>
<worksheet xml:id="unit-vector-practice">
  <title>Practice Problems</title>

  <exercise xml:id="unit-vector-practice-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        For each vector below, find a unit vector.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><p><m>\langle 6,-3\rangle</m></p></li>
        <li><p><m>\langle 5,0\rangle</m></p></li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="unit-vector-practice-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Two vectors represent velocities:
        <m>\mathbf{v}_1=\langle 10,-10\rangle</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{v}_2=\langle 2,-2\rangle</m>.
      </p>

      <ol marker="a.">
        <li>
          <p>
            Find a unit vector in the direction of each velocity.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            What do you notice about the two unit vectors?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            What does this tell you about how unit vectors encode direction?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="unit-vector-practice-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\mathbf{v}=\langle 3,4\rangle</m>.
      </p>

      <ol marker="a.">
        <li>
          <p>
            Find a unit vector <m>\mathbf{u}</m> in the direction of <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Write <m>\mathbf{v}</m> in the form <m>\lVert\mathbf{v}\rVert\,\mathbf{u}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Interpret this decomposition in words.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="unit-vector-practice-4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        A particle moves in the direction <m>\langle -1,2\rangle</m>.
      </p>

      <ol marker="a.">
        <li>
          <p>
            Find a unit vector that represents the direction of motion.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            If the speed of the particle is <m>5</m> units per second,
            write a velocity vector for the particle.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="unit-vector-practice-5">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Think about direction before computation. For each description,
        determine the axis and sign, then express the unit vector using
        <m>\mathbf{i}</m> and <m>\mathbf{j}</m>.
      </p>

      <ol marker="a.">
        <li><p>Write a unit vector pointing directly left.</p></li>
        <li><p>Write a unit vector pointing directly upward.</p></li>
        <li><p>Write a unit vector pointing directly downward.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="unit-vector-practice-6">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Decide whether each statement is <em>always true</em>,
        <em>sometimes true</em>, or <em>never true</em>.
        Explain briefly.
      </p>

      <ol marker="a.">
        <li>
          <p>
            Two vectors with the same magnitude must have the same unit vector.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Two vectors with the same unit vector must have the same magnitude.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Scaling a vector by a negative number changes its unit vector.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
      </subsection>
    </section>

